  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 1225 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nCurrent upper-air analysis and water vapor imagery depict the \nsurface low over northeast MT/northwest ND and a cold front pushing \nsoutheast across the Dakotas and into NE. Satellite imagery \nindicates partly cloudy skies behind the front as it moves out of \nthe region. Surface observations show temperatures mainly in the \nupper 60s and into 70s, with dewpoints in the 50s and 60s. West-\nnorthwesterly winds are gusting up to ~40kts across much of \nnorthwest SD and the plains to the east of the Black Hills and could \nreach as high as 50 to 55kts today. A wind advisory is in effect \nfrom 14/17z to 15/03z. These conditions will bring extreme fire \ndanger to northwest SD, with any fires that start being capable of a \nvery high to extreme rate of spread. One factor that will help in \nthe suppression of fire potential today is the precipitation that \nfell this morning over portions of northwest SD, with some areas \nseeing ~0.5\" of rain, though drier portions of the fire weather area \noutside the rain-affected zone remain most at risk.\n\nElsewhere, winds will be breezy behind the frontal passage, \ngenerally gusting 25 to 35kts, and will slowly diminish overnight, \nbecoming lighter by early morning. Temperatures will stay near \nseasonal, with highs mainly in the 70s with parts of southwestern \nand south-central SD reaching the 80s. Precipitation will exit the \nregion over the next few hours, leading to drier conditions for the \nrest of the day and into Tuesday.\n\nLooking ahead, temperatures will be a roller coaster as the jet \nstream wiggles back and forth over the region, with highs bouncing \nbetween the 70s and 80s in a typical summer-to-fall transition. \nBreezy conditions will be common most days, and multiple weak \nprecipitation chances will pass through, though meaningful rainfall \nmay be limited. Elevated fire weather conditions will remain a \nconcern across northeast WY and parts of southwest SD through the \nweek given the breezy pattern, continued drought conditions, and \nlimited rainfall. This weekend currently looks like the best shot \nat meaningful precipitation as an upper-level disturbance moves \nover the region. However, this is several days out and details may\nchange.",
